{
  "id": 508302634,
  "iid": 25803,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "opcua: Fix heap-use-after-free and missing padding validation in verify_padding",
  "description": "## Summary\n\nFixes a heap-use-after-free (out-of-bounds backwards read) in verify_padding() and a pre-existing invalid-padding fallthrough in decrypt_opcua() in plugins/epan/opcua/opcua.c, found via local ASan-instrumented fuzzing.\n\n## Root cause\n\n```c\nstatic int verify_padding(const uint8_t *padding)\n{\n    uint8_t pad_len = *padding;\n    for (uint8_t i = 0; i < pad_len; ++i) {\n        if (padding[-pad_len + i] != pad_len) return -1;\n    }\n    return pad_len;\n}\n```\n\nCalled as verify_padding(&plaintext\\[plaintext_len - \\*sig_len - 1\\]). With i = 0, the first loop access is padding\\[-pad_len\\] = plaintext\\[plaintext_len - sig_len - 1 - pad_len\\]. There is no check that this stays within plaintext\\[0 .. plaintext_len-1\\].\n\nSecondly, decrypt_opcua did not early-return when verify_padding returned -1. It fell through, wrote the attacker-controlled pad_len byte into \\*padding_len, and returned 0.\n\n## Fix\n\nAdd an available parameter to verify_padding carrying the number of bytes that safely precede padding in the buffer (i.e. pad_offset = plaintext_len - sig_len - 1), and reject any pad_len \\> available before the loop. No memory is read if the check fails.\n\nIn decrypt_opcua add a size guard (plaintext_len \\< sig_len + 1) to prevent unsigned underflow in the pad_offset computation, pass pad_offset as available; and add a return -1 on the invalid-padding path so the caller's if (ret == 0) check actually gates the payload_len arithmetic.\n\n## How this was found\n\nBuilt locally with Clang + ASan (-DENABLE_ASAN=1), then ran tools/fuzz-test.sh against the existing samples in test/captures/. Crashed on opcua-encrypted_with_chunking_with_keys.pcapng at pass 9:\n\n==310306==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free on address 0x50d0000214f3 READ of size 1 at 0x50d0000214f3 thread T0 #0 verify_padding plugins/epan/opcua/opcua.c:328 #1 decrypt_opcua plugins/epan/opcua/opcua.c:483 #2 dissect_opcua_message plugins/epan/opcua/opcua.c:654 0x50d0000214f3 is located 131 bytes inside of 144-byte region freed by thread T0 here: #0 free #1 g_bytes_unref #2 fvalue_cleanup epan/ftypes/ftypes.c:555 #3 proto_tree_free_node epan/proto.c:921 #4 proto_tree_reset epan/proto.c:931 #5 epan_dissect_reset epan/epan.c:727\n\nConfirmed deterministic every run. After the fix the same capture dissects cleanly with no ASan errors.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Rebuilt with ninja and re-ran tshark -nVxr against the crashing capture, clean output, no ASan abort\n- Re-ran tools/fuzz-test.sh against all test/captures/opcua\\*.pcap\\* samples; no regressions\n- Confirmed with ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0\n\nAI-Assisted: Yes (Claude), used to help set up the ASan/UBSan fuzzing environment.",
